  "result": "{\"seen_before\": true, \"matches\": [\"WSL#4851\", \"WSL#9508\"], \"answer\": \"This looks like a known limitation of the WSL2 localhost relay. Your k3s API server listens on a dual-stack wildcard socket (`*:6443`). In the archived tickets, the relay binds only `::1` on the Windows side for that kind of listener. So `localhost` resolves to `::1` and connects, but `127.0.0.1` is refused. WSL#4851 describes this exactly, with a server on `::` reachable by `localhost` but not by `127.0.0.1`. WSL#9508 shows the same pattern, where only IPv6 `::1` is forwarded and `curl -4`/127.0.0.1 is refused. I found no ticket that ties this to `wsl --shutdown`, and I found no fix in the archive. Workarounds from those tickets:\\n\\n- Make the service listen on IPv4 explicitly. Use `0.0.0.0` or `127.0.0.1`, for example by setting the k3s bind address to `0.0.0.0`. With an IPv4 listener, 127.0.0.1 is forwarded.\\n- Point Windows clients such as Headlamp at `https://localhost:6443` or `https://[::1]:6443`. Change the server address in the kubeconfig that Windows uses. Hosts-file entries can use `::1` instead of `127.0.0.1`.\\n- Alternatively, run the process IPv4-only. For Java that is `-Djava.net.preferIPv4Stack=true`. The equivalent for your service would be an IPv4-only listen address.\\n\\nBecause the k3s certificate is issued for specific SANs, check that `localhost` or `::1` is covered if you change the address.\"}",
